chine translation so  in the area of what is called content  localization so you think of  uh amazon being available not only in  english us or other english-speaking  languages  being available in all the other  countries around the world  uh all the information that needs to be  translated  to enable those customers to access data  in their own languages i think it would  be  impossible to translate the vast amounts  of information  behind the entire amazon  organization using professional  translators  there are just not enough professional  translators on the planet to do  that right i think  other areas where machine translation  now play a significant role  uh customer engagement in call centers  customer chats so in the past  if you lived in germany or in japan and  if you  wanted to have a conversation with a  customer support agent  that customer agent had to speak  japanese for example or had to speak  german  and there are just fewer people who  speak japanese and  fewer people who speak german than  people who speak for example  english so today  lots of those customer support or  customer engagement  are lots of behaviors are happening via  machine translation  um e-discovery i think it's a it's a  it's a big field  uh going from vast amounts of data  available in  multiple languages to drawing insights  um processing all that data  um alexa to certain extent right so  making alexa available in all the  languages  also machine translation plays an  important role  in accelerating the way in which we  deploy alexa across many languages  and so i think those are just a handful  of the  places where machine translation has  fundamentally changed  the world and now you also asked about  the science i think yeah if you could  share  the the the science i think when when  you look at the current  where the field is today improving  accuracy  is something that has been going on for  the last for the last 50 years in  machine translation  and we are not done yet there  so the field still continues to  work on improving the accuracy and that  is is  particularly important for  what is called low density languages  languages that  do not have that much data so if you  look at languages like spanish and  french  there are tens of thousands of  professional translators available in  those languages  billions and billions of words that  those languages  have in parallel data meaning translated  data  produced by professional translators so  it's so let's say building good  translation systems for spanish and  english and french  that are high density languages  just because data is there is much  easier than doing that for  languages that have  very little parallel data  very little very few examples of  human translated data or in some means  is maybe just  spoken language right so some of the  languages have  started to develop a writing system only  recently and to develop systems for  for those uh it's it's uh challenging so  you you mentioned english spanish and  french  um do you do you find those same  parallels for things that don't use the  traditional latin alphabet that we see  um do you do you find that i mean  obviously there's tons of translators  for things like  chinese and japanese or even russian  which uses a cyrillic alphabet  do you do you find that the same is kind  of true there where  languages that are broadly spoken  generally follow the same pattern  or do you find that there maybe are  interesting challenges that  chinese or japanese might present that  things like english and french and  spanish don't  i think it has less to do with the  alphabet or the  whether they use cyrillic or arabic or  chinese or characters or katakana  i think it has less to do with that  and it has more to do with how  divergent the languages are  in terms of the way they structure  information the way they pack  information so  english french spanish  they are pretty much the same language  just different characters different  words  all of them subject verb object  yes some have more morphology but uh  it's um they're much closer than let's  say when you look at  english and japanese or english and  korean  so that poses a little bit more  challenges because lots of the  information  in japanese sentences is only implicit  for example the  so to figure out or to build the  models that figure out that implicit  information or  even more difficult when you translate  into japanese to  figure out how to represent or to pre  implicitly to right to kind of  capture the to transfer the information  it's a little bit more challenging  than just going from french to spanish  or from french to english or vice versa  it's interesting you say that because i  would imagine you and and and folks that  have  backgrounds similar to yours you look at  languages like  french spanish even german uh and  english  and you say those are the same thing but  i will tell you that my  my 16 year old daughter who's taking  spanish would completely disagree with  you  uh and i think that i think most people  would but i can see when you look at it  from a pattern matching perspective  uh from a machine learning perspective  it's much easier to identify  those kinds of languages versus things  that use different structures i know  justin jeffers who's on my team  is is pretty fluent in japanese as well  as english  and he tried to explain to me once just  how the sentence structure works and how  you have to think about rearranging  um the language entirely and it is quite  different and so it's  uh it's interest i don't think most  people get an opportunity to look at  languages the way that you're describing  them so i think  that's awesome that you were able to  bring that up thank you  uh heart we're running short on time we  actually have six minutes left  uh and i have a whole list of questions  that i really wanted to get through with  you here  so there's one thing i really want to  touch on um i kno